WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.352
Error handling should help operators understand failures without exposing internals to users.

00:00:05.352 --> 00:00:10.165
Production responses should avoid stack traces, internal file paths, dependency versions,

00:00:10.165 --> 00:00:12.354
database messages, and implementation details.

00:00:12.354 --> 00:00:17.605
A user can receive a clear error category and request identifier while

00:00:17.605 --> 00:00:21.544
the detailed diagnostic information remains in protected operational systems.

00:00:21.544 --> 00:00:26.358
Structured logging helps teams search, alert, and investigate behavior across services.

00:00:26.358 --> 00:00:31.609
Useful fields may include request IDs, user or tenant identifiers where appropriate,

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:35.985
route names, status codes, latency, dependency failures, and security-relevant events.

00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:40.362
The logging design should avoid secrets, session tokens, authorization headers,

00:00:40.362 --> 00:00:42.550
password values, raw sensitive payloads,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.926
and personally sensitive data that is not needed for operations.

00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:50.427
Observability is also part of incident response readiness.

00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:51.302
A Node.

00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:56.116
js service should make it possible to notice unusual authentication failures,

00:00:56.116 --> 00:00:59.617
authorization denials, validation failures, dependency warnings, rate-limit events,

00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.243
unexpected outbound requests, and runtime errors.

00:01:02.243 --> 00:01:08.807
Dependency and runtime warnings should not be ignored simply because the service appears to work.

00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:11.871
They may signal security or reliability drift.

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:17.560
The balance is to understand behavior without turning logs into a data leak.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:22.374
Logs often travel to third-party platforms, shared dashboards, long-term storage, backups,

00:01:22.374 --> 00:01:23.686
or developer laptops.

00:01:23.686 --> 00:01:26.312
Treat logs as sensitive operational data.

00:01:26.312 --> 00:01:31.564
Good observability helps teams detect and respond to problems while preserving privacy,

00:01:31.564 --> 00:01:35.940
protecting secrets, and keeping production details out of untrusted responses.
